// https://cdn.opencores.org/downloads/wbspec_b4.pdf
// Illustration 3-13
ADR[31:0]    X 0x1111 0x2222 0x2222      X X
DAT_I[31:0]  X      X      X      X      X X
DAT_O[31:0]  X 0xffff 0xaaaa 0xaaaa      X X
// Note: it is unclear whether WE actually needs to be 1 during the last ack here.
//       The manual says (2.2.3):
//       > The write enable output [WE_O] indicates whether the current local bus cycle
//       > is a READ or WRITE
//       Which would support this waveform, i.e., WE has to be held at 1 or 0
//       while CYC is asserted. However, in Illustration 3-14 (RMW cycle),
//       WE_O does change while CYC is asserted.
//       On the other hand (Rule 3.60):
//       > [client] interfaces MUST qualify the following signals with [STB_O]: [ADR_O],
//       > [DAT_O()], [SEL_O()], [WE_O], and [TAGN_O].
//       Which seems to imply that if STB is low, the value of WE does not matter.
WE           X      1      1      1      X X
SEL[3:0]     X    0xf    0xf    0xf      X X
STB          0      1      1      1      0 0
ACK          0      0      1      0      1 0
CYC          0      1      1      1      1 0
STALL        0      0      1      0      0 0
// extra pins not included in the original illustration
RST          0      0      0      0      0 0
ERR          0      0      0      0      0 0
RTY          0      0      0      0      0 0
